rem $ Key to the species of Enteridium from Taiwan (inspired by Martin and Alexopoulos, 1969)
1,0 $ Pseudocapillitium membranous at base (but not perforated), fraying out into dendroid columns of flexuous threads; peridium persistent; aethalia often large, up to 3 cm or more inextent … $ E. lycoperdon
1,0 $ Pseudocapillitium composed entirely of dendroid columns; peridium thin, brittle, evanescent; aethalia rarely exceeding 2 cm in extent $ E. intermedium

cs2,0$

rem $ Key to the species of Lycogala from Taiwan (inspired by Martin and Alexopoulos, 1969; Ing, 1999)
1,0 $ Aethalia large, 2 cm or more in diameter; peridium smooth or nearly so, without scales $ L. flavofuscum
1,2 $ Aethalia less than 2 cm in diameter; peridium with scales or warts
2,0 $ Aethalia conical, taller than broad $ L. conicum
2,3 $ Aethalia subglobose or pulvinate, not notably taller than broad
3,0 $ Aethalia pallid to yellow-brown, rarely blackish, mostly 3-15 mm in diameter; cortical scales neither strongly pustulate nor tessellate; pseudocapillitium strongly sculptured, 6-25 µm in diameter $ L. epidendrum
3,0 $ Aethalia brownish ochraceous to dark brown or black, mostly 2.5-5.5 mm in diameter, rarely larger; cortical scales pulvinate, prominent, dark, at first pustulate, tending to become tessellate; pseudocapillitium not strongly sculptured, 2-5 µm in diameter $L. exiguum

